- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.

Rule:
- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.
- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="9ba4a9aa" lines="75-90">
<description>
Return the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.

Input structure
- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,
  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.

Rule
- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.
- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.
- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.

Key insight
- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide
  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="9b30e358" lines="9-23">
<description>
Repeat the bottom foreground motif upward until it fills the whole grid.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground pixels occupy a contiguous block of rows at the bottom.
- That bottom block is the complete vertical motif, including its background cells.

Rule
- Copy the bottom motif rows repeatedly upward across the full grid.
- Preserve horizontal alignment and vertical phase: the original first motif row remains at its input row.
- The output shape is unchanged.

Key insight
The blank rows above the bottom foreground block are missing repetitions of the same row motif, not empty space.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="581f7754" lines="8-22">
<description>
Slide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color "marker" pixel.
- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.

Rule:
- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.
- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.
- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.

Key insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="80214e03" lines="48-64">
<description>
Extract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.

Input structure:
- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.
- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.

Rule:
- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.
- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.
- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.

Key insight:
- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="538b439f" lines="122-142">
<description>
Reflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel
  separator line in a non-background color.
- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.
- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.

Rule:
- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.
- Mirror each source object across the separator line.
- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the
  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the
  reflected object in the source color.

Key insight:
- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified
  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while
  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="2ccd9fef" lines="173-187">
<description>
Predict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.

Input structure
- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.
- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.
- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.

Rule
- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.
- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.
- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.

Key insight
- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="2b9ef948" lines="51-65">
<description>
Move the yellow 3x3 frame by the displacement encoded by the gray path, then draw its diagonal yellow corner rays.

Input structure:
